{{also|Linn|linn-}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/lɪn/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪn|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{der|en|gd|-}} or {{der|en|ga|linn}}, conflated to some extent with {{m|en|linn||waterfall}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}
[[File:Leehouse Linn - geograph.org.uk - 2727785.jpg|thumb|A '''linn''', the [[turbulent]] pond fed and formed by the falling waters of a [[cascade]]]]
[[File:20190417 Taroko Gorge-23.jpg|thumb|This [[ravine]] in Taiwan meets the definition of a '''linn''' in the sense of a steep-walled ravine]]

# {{lb|en|Scotland|Northern England|Wales}} A [[pool#Noun|pool]] of [[water#Noun|water]], especially one formed and agitated by the water from a [[cascade]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1812|journal=Forbes|title=Poems|section=49
|passage=There frisks the freckl'd finny tribe,<br>In '''linns''' both wide and steep.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1823|author=Galt|title=Gilhaize|section=xxviii
|passage=In the clear '''linn''' the trouts shuttled from stone and crevice.}}
#* '''1868''' September 24, James Hardy, addressed delivered at Chirnside, quoted in the ''History of the Berwickshire Naturalists' Club'', volume 5, page 386:
#*: The pool is there — the true '''linn''', in the original acceptance of the word — dark and bottomless.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1885|author=Robert Louis Stevenson|title=Prince Otto
|passage=A [[trellis]]ed path led down into the valley of the [[brook]], and he turned to follow it. The stream was a [[breakneck]], boiling Highland river. [[hard by|Hard by]] the farm, it leaped a little [[precipice]] in a thick grey-mare's tail of twisted [[filament]]s, and then lay and worked and bubbled in a ''' linn'''. Into the middle of this [[quake|quaking]] pool a rock [[protrude]]d, shelving to a cape; and [[thither]] Otto scrambled and sat down to [[ponder]].}}
#* '''1894''', Haliburton, ''Furth'', 177:
#*: His successful angler landing the '''linn'''-lier [fish that inhabits a pool of water].
#* '''1896''', Crockett, ''Grey Man'', vii:
#*: The running of deep water in a '''linn'''.
# {{lb|en|Scotland|Northern England|Wales}} A [[torrent]] of [[water#Noun|water]] running over a rocky bed.

